The following options are target specific:
  -fbaserel                   data is addressed relative to a4
  -fbaserel32                 data is addressed relative to a4 with 32 bit
                              offsets
  -fbbb=                      Enable Bebbo's optimizations.
                              +    enable all optimizations (default)
                              a    commute add move instructions
                              b    use register for base addresses
                              c    convert load const and compare into a sub
                              e    eliminate dead assignments + redundant loads
                              f    shrink stack frame
                              h    optimize shift instructions
                              i    use post increment on addresses
                              l    remove single used lea if possible
                              n    pipeline instructions
                              m    merge add and move statements
                              p    propagate move assignment pairs out of loops
                              r    register renaming to maybe save registers
                              s    a strcpy optimization
                              z    final pass with simple substitutions
                              0    insert clear registers before storing a byte
                              or word, if possible
                              v    be verbose
                              V    be very verbose
                              x    dump insns
                              Default: -fbbb=+ which yields
                              -fbbb=abcefhilmnprsz0
  -fdouble-indirect           Use double indirect addressing mode.
  -fno-truncz-same-mode       emit round to zero insns (e.g. fintrz.x fp0,fp0)
  -m5200                      Generate code for a 520X.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -m5206e                     Generate code for a 5206e.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -m528x                      Generate code for a 528x.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -m5307                      Generate code for a 5307.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -m5407                      Generate code for a 5407.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -m68000                     Generate code for a 68000.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -m68010                     Generate code for a 68010.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -m68020                     Generate code for a 68020.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -m68020-40                  Generate code for a 68040, without any new
                              instructions.
  -m68020-60                  Generate code for a 68060, without any new
                              instructions.
  -m68030                     Generate code for a 68030.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -m68040                     Generate code for a 68040.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -m68060                     Generate code for a 68060.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -m68080                     Generate code for a 68080.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -m68302                     Generate code for a 68302.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -m68332                     Generate code for a 68332.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -m68851                     Generate code for a 68851.
  -m68881                     Generate code that uses 68881 floating-point
                              instructions.
  -malign-int                 Align variables on a 32-bit boundary.
  -march=                     Specify the name of the target architecture.
  -mbitfield                  Use the bit-field instructions.
  -mc68000                    Generate code for a 68000.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -mc68020                    Generate code for a 68020.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -mcfv4e                     Generate code for a ColdFire v4e.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -mcpu32                     Generate code for a cpu32.  Same as -mcpu=.
  -mcpu=                      Specify the target CPU.
  -mcrt=                      Specify startup binary
  -mdiv                       Use hardware division instructions on ColdFire.
  -mexperimental              experimental code, e.g. using new 68080
                              instructions, registers, etc.p.p.
  -mfidoa                     Generate code for a Fido A.
  -mhard-float                Generate code which uses hardware floating point
                              instructions.
  -mid-shared-library         Enable ID based shared library.
  -mnobitfield                Do not use the bit-field instructions.
  -mnortd                     Use normal calling convention.
  -mnoshort                   Consider type 'int' to be 32 bits wide.
  -mpcrel                     Generate pc-relative code.
  -mregparm                   Same as -mregparm=2  Same as -mregparm=.
  -mregparm=                  Pass arguments through registers.
  -mrtd                       Use different calling convention using 'rtd'.
  -msasregparm                Same as -mregparm=2 but do not use alternate
                              registers
  -mschulz                    Don't use fmove.x, used fmove.d instead
  -msep-data                  Enable separate data segment.
  -mshared-library-id=        ID of shared library to build.
  -mshort                     Consider type 'int' to be 16 bits wide.
  -msmall-code                small code model
  -msoft-float                Generate code with library calls for floating
                              point.
  -mstrict-align              Do not use unaligned memory references.
  -mtune=                     Tune for the specified target CPU or architecture.
  -mxgot                      Support more than 8192 GOT entries on ColdFire.
  -mxtls                      Support TLS segment larger than 64K.
  -noixemul                   Do not use ixemul.library - use libnix instead to
                              link
  -ramiga-dev                 Use devinit.o as start file
  -ramiga-lib                 Use libinit.o as start file
  -ramiga-libr                Use libinitr.o as start file
  -resident                   data is addressed relative to a4, linked as
                              resident
  -resident32                 data is addressed relative to a4 with 32 bit
                              offsets, linked as resident

  Known M68K ISAs (for use with the -march= option):
    68000 68010 68020 68030 68040 68060 68080 cpu32 isaa isaaplus isab isac

  Known M68K CPUs (for use with the -mcpu= option):
    51 51ac 51ag 51cn 51em 51je 51jf 51jg 51jm 51mm 51qe 51qm 5202 5204 5206
    5206e 5207 5208 5210a 5211 5211a 5212 5213 5214 5216 5221x 52221 52223 52230
    52231 52232 52233 52234 52235 5224 5225 52252 52254 52255 52256 52258 52259
    52274 52277 5232 5233 5234 5235 523x 5249 5250 5253 5270 5271 5272 5274 5275
    5280 5281 5282 528x 53011 53012 53013 53014 53015 53016 53017 5307 5327 5328
    5329 532x 5372 5373 537x 5407 54410 54415 54416 54417 54418 54450 54451
    54452 54453 54454 54455 5470 5471 5472 5473 5474 5475 547x 5480 5481 5482
    5483 5484 5485 548x 68000 68010 68020 68030 68040 68060 68080 68302 68332
    cpu32 fidoa

  Known M68K microarchitectures (for use with the -mtune= option):
    68000 68010 68020 68020-40 68020-60 68020-80 68030 68040 68060 68080 cfv1
    cfv2 cfv3 cfv4 cfv4e cpu32

